WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.701
-It's so great to see you.
-Great to see you.

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:03.801
-And yesterday,
it was your birthday.

00:00:03.801 --> 00:00:04.901
Happy birthday.
-Thank you very much.

00:00:04.901 --> 00:00:07.367
Thank you very much. Thank you.

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:12.067
-I watch you -- I want to say,
I've watched you all day today.

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:13.300
I watched you --
-Well --

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.367
-Right?

00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:17.033
-So, the deal is,
I started at 6:00 a.m.

00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:19.133
on "Morning Joe,"
went till 10:00 a.m.

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:20.167
Then I went across the street,

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.767
co-hosted with Hoda
for hour five.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:25.734
And now, I'm here tonight
shutting it down.

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:28.767
-Exactly. All day long.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:31.367
This guy -- He's a workhorse.
Come on. NBC!

00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:34.868
-So, basically, the deal is --
and you may not know this --

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:38.033
there's a key under the mat,
right on 50th Street.

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.067
I open up in the morning.

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.100
-And you're going to
lock up tonight.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.467
-And then, tonight,
I'll sweep up,

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:42.734
put the stools back on the bar,

00:00:42.734 --> 00:00:44.234
and when you're done,
we'll all go home.

00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:46.167
-I've always wanted to do that,
but you actually just did it.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:47.934
I mean, I watch you on
"Morning Joe" every morning.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:48.968
You're fantastic.

00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:50.234
The whole crew
over there is great.

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:51.534
But then, "Today" show --
You know, "Today" show

00:00:51.534 --> 00:00:53.467
has been on --
I keep seeing this --

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:54.534
70-year anniversary.

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:55.601
70 years.

00:00:55.601 --> 00:00:57.801
-70 years. 70 years.
-That's amazing.

00:00:57.801 --> 00:00:59.434
-I know. Isn't that crazy? Yeah.

00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:01.801
-A great team
over there, as well.

00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:03.400
-I've been there 10 years.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.767
10 years,
which is an amazing thing.

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:06.167
You probably have this
a little bit, too,

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:09.734
where you grew up watching
"SNL" or Johnny Carson,

00:01:09.734 --> 00:01:12.200
and you can't believe
you're sitting in this seat.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.501
And I had a little of that
in the "Today" show.

00:01:13.501 --> 00:01:14.767
It's like you're a kid,
and then,

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:16.100
at some point in your life,
you stepped through

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.501
the television, and you're the
one on the other side

00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:19.734
that's hosting the "Today" show.

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:22.501
It's a wild thing.
-Yeah. You do a great job.

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:24.400
-Thanks, man.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.367
-I want to
ask you about something,

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:28.801
because I saw you do recently --
You ran the marathon.

00:01:28.801 --> 00:01:31.000
-Yes.
-This was a major deal.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.968
Dude, you were great.
I go -- Are you a runner?

00:01:33.968 --> 00:01:35.300
-I'm not a runner at all.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.767
Matter of fact, I was, like --

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.300
The turkey trot at Thanksgiving
was as far as I would go,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.400
like that 5K
or the 1-mile, even.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.534
But during COVID,
needed a little purpose, right?

00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:45.067
You wanted to do something.

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:47.601
And I decided to run for
the Michael J. Fox Foundation.

00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:50.701
-Ah, that's my guy.
-Yep.

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:51.801
-Do you have any --

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:54.734
-My dad has had
Parkinson's disease

00:01:54.734 --> 00:01:56.901
for about 30 years,
unfortunately.

00:01:56.901 --> 00:01:59.033
So, I'm on the board of
the Michael J. Fox Foundation.

00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:01.701
I said, "Let's go raise some
money and see what we can do."

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:04.067
And I set out and I trained for
months and months and months.

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:05.968
And I ran it under four hours

00:02:05.968 --> 00:02:09.400
and raised about half-a-million
bucks for research.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.200
-$500,000. Good for you.
-Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.133
-You know what
I loved about this is that

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:17.400
I saw a story on you
running this,

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.667
and I can't even imagine
running three blocks.

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:25.501
But I know that you wrote "dad"
on your arm for anytime

00:02:25.501 --> 00:02:26.534
that you're like, "I gotta quit.

00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:27.567
This is crazy."

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:28.901
-Yeah, I mean, 26 miles,

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.234
especially if you're not
a runner, is no joke.

00:02:30.234 --> 00:02:31.534
And there are points --

00:02:31.534 --> 00:02:32.901
They tell you about the walls
you're going to hit,

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:33.968
and those are very real.

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:36.234
So, you hit like 16 miles
in the Bronx.

00:02:36.234 --> 00:02:38.300
You say, "I can't do
10 more miles or 20 miles.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.634
I can't do 4 more miles."
-Can't do it.

00:02:39.634 --> 00:02:42.167
-So you look down and you
remember why you're doing it.

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:44.667
And if it's not going to
save your dad,

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:46.367
it might save
the next generation

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:48.400
and find a cure
and give money to research.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.434
So that was pure motivation
was my dad swept me across

00:02:51.434 --> 00:02:52.767
the finish line,
without question.

00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:54.734
-Yeah, that was great, bud.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:54.734 --> 00:02:57.300
That was pretty cool.
I liked that you had that.

00:02:58.801 --> 00:03:02.167
You said the toughest -- One of
the toughest parts is going --

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:03.434
Is it Fifth Avenue?

00:03:03.434 --> 00:03:06.100
-Fifth Avenue, yep.
-Because, that's --

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.334
-People don't realize --
When you're in a taxi,

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:09.767
Fifth Avenue feels real flat.

00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:10.968
-Yeah.
-It's not.

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:13.567
-It's not flat.
-It's uphill. It is uphill.

00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:15.601
And I was running uphill.

00:03:15.601 --> 00:03:17.100
I guess that's Mile 24,
something like that.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.400
-Whew!

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.634
-And you're really, like,
you've hit the wall totally.

00:03:20.634 --> 00:03:22.767
Like, "I don't think I can even
get into the park."

00:03:22.767 --> 00:03:25.734
And I had this amazing moment
where I looked up,

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:28.534
and Michael J. Fox
was standing outside

00:03:28.534 --> 00:03:30.968
his apartment building
on Fifth Avenue.

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:34.100
He came out. He had his cane.
He was in his leather jacket.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.901
And I needed it
and I broke into a sprint

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:41.200
and went over
and gave him a big, sweaty hug.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.133
And, truly, that made the --
That took me home.

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:47.601
It took me home.
-Yeah.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:49.434
We have a video of
Michael J. Fox

00:03:49.434 --> 00:03:50.834
giving you a hug mid-race.

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:52.000
Take a look at this right here.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.901
-That's it, Willie!

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:05.400
-That was a tear, by the way.
That was a tear.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.634
I was wiping away a tear.

00:04:06.634 --> 00:04:08.767
-That wasn't sweat, no.
-No, that was a tear.

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:12.801
And he just means so much to so
many people, to our family.

00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:14.701
He's been an inspiration to
a million people

00:04:14.701 --> 00:04:16.400
in this country who have
Parkinson's disease.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.701
He's the guy.

00:04:17.701 --> 00:04:19.834
He's the reason you keep going
because he's kept going,

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:21.067
and so that was a huge moment.

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:24.467
-He's a legend, yeah.
We love Michael J. Fox.

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:27.067
Here's you crossing
the finish line.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.968
And I go, "Man, you look great."

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:31.601
And you -- I mean, seriously,
I mean, you look fantastic.

00:04:31.601 --> 00:04:35.033
And you told me --
You go, "Yeah, thank you."

00:04:35.033 --> 00:04:36.100
Because I showed you the photo.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.234
I go, "We're going to
talk about this."

00:04:37.234 --> 00:04:38.534
And you go,
"Yeah, it actually isn't me

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:39.868
crossing the finish line."

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:41.400
And I go, "Well, that's
interesting, because it is."

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.234
-But if you look closely,
so, the clock says 4:10.

00:04:44.234 --> 00:04:46.734
My time was 3:58.
I want that on the record.

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:49.067
That should not be
the photograph of record.

00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:51.601
-You were -- So you broke --
You were under four hours.

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:53.067
-I was under four.
So, here's what happened.

00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:54.367
I finished under four.

00:04:54.367 --> 00:04:55.901
I couldn't believe --
Like, four was a lofty goal,

00:04:55.901 --> 00:04:57.100
and I did it.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.067
I'm sitting back in the tent
after the race,

00:04:59.067 --> 00:05:01.267
and this guy comes up,
and he says, "Mr. Geist --"

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.767
I mean, I'm crapped up.
I can't do anything.

00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:04.067
-Yeah.

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:06.033
-"Do you mind coming back out
to the finish line?"

00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:09.267
And I said, "I do, actually.
I can't move."

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:11.267
And he said, "We need you to
re-enact the finish."

00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:12.634
And I said, "Why?"

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:15.334
He said, "Well, I'm going to be
real honest with you.

00:05:15.334 --> 00:05:19.667
Chelsea Clinton was crossing
right about when you were.

00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:21.067
Former president Clinton,

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:22.300
former secretary of state
Clinton

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.734
were waiting at the finish line.

00:05:23.734 --> 00:05:28.267
All of our cameras were on that,
and no one took your picture."

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:30.634
[ Sad tuba plays ]

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:33.834
Yeah. No one took a picture.

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:39.634
So, I limp back out,
like a salmon going upstream,

00:05:39.634 --> 00:05:40.868
while everyone's finishing.

00:05:40.868 --> 00:05:43.767
-This is -- Whoever ran
two marathons at once?

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:47.934
Willie Geist, the first man ever
to run two marathons in one day.

00:05:47.934 --> 00:05:49.701
-And they're like,
"Keep going back farther."

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:51.000
And the announcer is like,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.667
"Ladies and gentlemen,
look who's back. Willie --"

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:53.934
I'm like, "Oh, no! No!"

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:56.100
-You're like, "Oh, my gosh.
This is a disaster."

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.501
-So, I crossed it again,
and that's the photo I have,

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:00.100
framed with the wrong time.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:01.501
But shout-out to
Chelsea Clinton.

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:03.634
She did great.
-Yeah. I love that you did it.

00:06:03.634 --> 00:06:06.067
Hey, you know, I love you
on "Sunday Today," as well,

00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:08.601
because I get to see
more Willie Geist.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:09.968
And you do some good
long-form interviews on there.

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:11.167
-Yeah, it's fun.

00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:13.267
You've been on the show
for one of your many

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:14.767
number-1 best-selling
children's books.

00:06:14.767 --> 00:06:15.901
-I want to come back.

00:06:15.901 --> 00:06:17.601
I want to come back
for anything, bud.

00:06:17.601 --> 00:06:20.367
-A couple weeks ago, we had
Sienna Miller on, who we love.

00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:21.400
Sienna Miller is great.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.567
-She's just
an unbelievable actor.

00:06:23.567 --> 00:06:25.934
-And so cool
and smart and funny.

00:06:25.934 --> 00:06:28.968
And she was -- We were doing it
in the restaurant of a hotel,

00:06:28.968 --> 00:06:29.968
the interview.

00:06:29.968 --> 00:06:31.033
It was just the two of us.

00:06:31.033 --> 00:06:33.133
All these cameras
and the lights like this.

00:06:33.133 --> 00:06:35.567
And there was
a convention at the hotel.

00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:38.968
So groups were coming down
with their lanyards on,

00:06:38.968 --> 00:06:40.801
and they would
walk into this interview

00:06:40.801 --> 00:06:41.901
and they go, "Excuse us.

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:43.667
Is this the breakfast?"

00:06:43.667 --> 00:06:46.701
And we're like,
"No, there are seven cameras

00:06:46.701 --> 00:06:49.100
and studio lighting and
Sienna Miller."

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:50.501
They'd be like, "We're sorry."

00:06:50.501 --> 00:06:55.133
And then like two minutes later,
"Hi, is this the breakfast?"

00:06:56.467 --> 00:06:58.033
It would be like
if someone walked in here

00:06:58.033 --> 00:06:59.868
and was like,
"Is this the Rainbow Room?"

00:06:59.868 --> 00:07:02.334
And you'd be like, "No, this is
'The Tonight Show.'"

00:07:02.334 --> 00:07:03.834
-Yeah. You're always the best.
-Great to see you, brother.

00:07:03.834 --> 00:07:05.534
-Willie Geist.
-Thank you.

00:07:05.534 --> 00:07:06.634
Willie Geist.

00:07:06.634 --> 00:07:07.934
"Sunday Today with Willie Geist"

00:07:07.934 --> 00:07:10.634
airs every Sunday
at 8:00 a.m. on NBC.

